  "oneLiner": "A concurrent Set game where independent player threads share a dealer-controlled table.",
  "summary": "A Java concurrency assignment turns the card game into a coordination problem: keyboard and computer players submit selections while a dealer validates sets, awards points, replaces cards and manages the round timer. The engineering story is the boundary between independent input, shared table state and centralized validation.",
  "stack": [
    "Java 8",
    "Threads, monitors and semaphores",
    "Swing / AWT",
    "Maven",
    "JUnit 5",
    "Mockito"
  ],
  "capabilityGroups": [
    {
      "title": "Independent players, one shared table",
      "summary": "Each player runs on a separate thread. Bounded input and candidate queues connect player actions to the dealer, while synchronized table operations maintain card and token state.",
      "details": [
        "Human input maps configured keys to player and slot.",
        "Computer players generate random key presses; they are not strategic solvers.",
        "The checked-in configuration starts four computer players and zero human players."
      ],
